<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#doge-of-venice">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Doge of Venice</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">697–<a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Doge, Dux Venetiarum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The doge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> was the highest authority of the Republic of Venice (697–1797), head of state and of the Venetian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>, elected for life. The title comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> dux, leader, and grew out of the offices of Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a>; the first historically attested doge was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orso-ipato">Orso Ipato</a> in the first half of the 8th century. From 1268 the doge was chosen by a complex machinery of lots and nominating committees in the Great Council, meant to limit the influence of the great families, and he was kept under strict surveillance. As the oligarchy took over his functions the office became mostly representative. The last doge, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-manin">Ludovico Manin</a>, abdicated in 1797 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/doge-of-venice.jpg" alt="Doge of Venice" width="330" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernardino Castelli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lodovico_Manin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
